This test has been marked with the `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attribute which denotes it will use the underlying target method/property (in this case, the `Insert` method of the `ArrayList` class).
The `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue` method is used to ensure the preconditions for the test scenario, i.e., the `index` passed is within the range of the list indices.

The test creates copies of the input list state before and after calling the `Insert` method. After the method call, it compares the states of the input and output lists. In addition, the values related to the inserted item & its position in the list are observed. The observed data is used for test generation & verification.

The assertions in the code block are placeholders. You need to add the proper assertion logic for the test to pass. The first assertion checks that the last position of the item in the old and new lists are different. The second assertion verifies that the item is where it should be in the new list.

Notice that the `ArrayList` class is part of a framework (`Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting` and `Microsoft.Pex.Framework`) rather than a user-written class, hence should be tested as integration tests instead of unit tests.

After adding and executing this test, Pex will generate a new range of test cases to verify the logic of the `Insert` method to the extent that meets the test's requirements and constraints.

For more details about test generation, see [Testing with Pex: In-depth coverage – Part 1](https://blogs.microsoft.co.il/sasha/2013/06/20/testing-with-pex-in-depth-coverage-part-1/). And for more information about the `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attribute, see [The [PexAssumeUnderTest] Attribute in Pex](https://blogs.microsoft.co.il/sasha/2013/06/13/pexassumeundertest-attribute-in-pex/). 

In summary, this is how you can write a parameterized contract test in Pex for a method like `ArrayList.Insert(int, Object)` using `[PexClass]`, `[PexMethod]` and `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` attributes, and generate comprehensive test scenarios for your code under test. 

Pex will explore the method's logic, run tests for the different possible scenarios, and provide an accurate test suite to verify your code. It can also help you catch potential bugs by producing test inputs that fail the method under test. 

Hopefully, this gives you a good idea of how to use Pex to create tests for ArrayList.Test.

Remember that, while Pex can generate new test cases for you, it might not necessarily create the best possible test cases, particularly where the method under test has a complex logic that's hard to abstract or decompose. It's about creating tests that are fit for the target method rather than the problem to be solved.

