And now the test code:
```C#
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {

        [ContractTestMethod]
        public void PUT_InsertTest(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            Assume.True(index >= 0);
            Assume.True(arrList.Count > index || index == 0);
            Assume.True(arrList.Count <= index + 1); // index should not refer to an existing element

            Assume.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.Insert(index, x));
        }

    }
}
```
Please note that the code is only an example and the assumptions are quite strict (although they were made with a purpose to ensure that Pex will not generate invalid inputs). These assumptions are made to ensure that Pex will produce valid inputs, and thus more meaningful results.

The tests are simple and provide a basic view of what a testing strategy for a list's Insert() function might look like, although of course, more complex usage scenarios may require different handling. The Pex is only performing basic function verification. In a more complex test suite, there might be additional checks or assertions to ensure the list's correctness across various operations and states. 

The contract is a strong form of testing since it verifies the behavior of the system with respect to some assumptions about its behavior. In other words, when we use Pex for verification, we assume that the input values are well-formed and reasonable, and we want to check whether the system behaves properly. In other words, we want to validate the system in terms of its input and output interface. But as I mentioned earlier, these tests are quite naive, more complex cases might be needed for a fully accurate verification. Also, the tests do not cover some corner cases, you can add more assumptions to cover those cases. 

Also note that Pex only works with test method and not with contracts or other test frameworks. It is a Microsoft Research open-source project to increase code coverage for unit tests. But it is not a testing automation framework.

This might not be a perfect answer, but it should help give you some direction to consider with regards to unit testing with Pex in the context of your specific contract test scenario. ``` 
To use Pex in practice and test contracts, I would suggest to write more detailed, more specific unit tests than contract tests. Because, although Pex can find a lot of potential input values that can cause failures, it only focuses on edge and boundary cases. Therefore, a focus on writing a comprehensive range of test cases not just edge and boundary cases helps to increase the coverage and effectiveness of the tests. 

Also note that Pex is quite a new tool and as such, it might not always provide the highest quality or most effective tests. Thus, in the long term, I recommend considering the trade-offs between code coverage and test quality. 

Finally, although Pex has its limitations, it is still useful in situations where other automated means of testing aren’t viable. It can lead to quite a bit of time saved in terms of finding edge cases and in some complex scenarios manual testing might be necessary to discover issues that automated tests might miss. So depending on the project requirements, considering the time constraints, budget, skillset of the development team etc., it is critical to decide when to use Pex and when to use other testing methods.
 
