### <a name="BKMK_TestingCodeContracts"></a>


##  <a name="TestingCodeContracts"></a>Testing Code Contracts

Code contracts are a way to specify, in the code, that certain conditions are true before and after the execution of a method. When these conditions are not met, code contracts will generate an exception. 

Visual Studio provides tools for browsing and analyzing code contracts across your codebase. When you analyze your code, the Contract.Requires and Contract.Ensures contracts will be highlighted if any contract checking conditions fail. When you hover over a highlighted area, its message will be displayed in the VS status bar. 

The Contract.Requires method is used to specify preconditions. Preconditions are conditions that must be true before the method is invoked. The first parameter specifies the condition, and optional second parameter allows for a detailed message to be passed if the condition fails. 

For example, you could have a code contract as follows: 

```csharp
public void Add(int x)
{
    Contract.Requires(x != 0);
    // method's implementation
}
```

In this case, the code will throw an exception if a call to the Add method is made with a parameter of 0.

The Contract.Ensures method is used to specify postconditions. Postconditions are conditions that must be true after the method has been invoked. The first parameter specifies the condition, and optional second parameter allows for a detailed message to be passed if the condition fails. 

For example, 

```csharp
public int GetCount()
{
    Contract.Ensures(Contract.Result<int>() >= 0);
    // method's implementation
}
```

If GetCount method implementation changes the list count to be negative, an exception will be thrown.

Notice that in order to use Contract.Requires and Contract.Ensures you'll have to use a ContractClassAttribute on your class (or struct) indicating the type of the contract, like this: 

```csharp
[ContractClass(typeof(MyClassContract))]
public interface IMyClass
{
    void Add(int x);
    int GetCount();
}
```

And you would implement MyClassContract:

```csharp
[ContractClassFor(typeof(IMyClass))]
public abstract class MyClassContract : IMyClass
{
    public void Add(int x)
    {
        Contract.Requires(x != 0);
    }

    public int GetCount()
    {
        Contract.Ensures(Contract.Result<int>() >= 0);
        return 0; // just a placeholder, not actual implementation
    }
}
```

Visual Studio’s Code Contracts feature ensures that these conditions are not only be checked but can be tested as well by providing unit test methods that demonstrate that the contract conditions will never fail under normal circumstances. 

For more testing details, see [Test Generation of Code Contracts](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd264973.aspx) in MSDN.

##  <a name="BKMK_WhyCodeContractsCausesInteroperabilityProblems"></a>


##  <a name="CodeContractsCausesInteroperabilityProblems"></a>Why Code Contracts Causes Interoperability Problems 

Code contracts can cause interoperability problems due to two primary reasons:

1. Language Interoperation: Code contracts are a C# construct that relies on attributes and compiler support. While most modern languages support these features directly within the language, for the .NET Framework and older versions of languages, there's no built-in support. As a result, these constructs would not function as expected when applied to methods or properties in .NET Framework or older language compilers.

2. Interoperability Between C# and Other Languages: Code contracts are written and utilized within the C# language. When utilizing these constructs, you must translate them to the target language. The way you translate code contracts to non-C# languages can lead to problems, as this requires a deeper understanding of the specific language'--------------------            Assert.AreNotSame(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "Indexes");
        }
    }
}

Here is a simple explanation of this method:

The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to specify that the method should be tested with the supplied ArrayList argument.
The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is used to record a value that can be viewed in Visual Studio after the method call.
The method Insert is called on the ArrayList object with the supplied index and item.
The index parameter represents the insert point. The Count property gets incremented.
The Assert.AreNotSame method is used to validate that the expected and actual values are different.

The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is used to record the values that will be viewed if the test fails.

Note: Pex will not work if you're using a reference type as an argument (e.g., ArrayList), because Pex will not be able to get a copy of the object in the test method to mutate it. That's why we are using the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute. For this attribute to work, Pex will create a mutable copy of the ArrayList object which will be passed to the test method. The mutations that you make (to the object) will not be tracked by Pex.

Note: the "@@@last position of x in old and new lists are different" is a placeholder text and must be replaced with the actual assertion or condition that you wish to test. The text "@@@" is used as a marker on where to insert a placeholder for the variable name in the generated assertion string.

Note: In this example, the Pex-generated test method is used to verify Insert's post-condition: the list's last index of the element should change. However, it's recommended to use other post-conditions such as testing whether the element is in the list, the count of the list etc.

Note: In complex scenarios where array lists are involved, the test might sometimes fail due to the non-deterministic nature of the test execution order or other reasons. That's why it's recommended to write specific test methods and pre-conditions for complex methods like Insert. Pex is a good fit for unit testing when the code under test is easier to understand and smaller in scope. For complex cases use tools that are designed specifically for such cases like Code Contracts or ReSharper.Pex.

This is a basic PEX usage and might not cover all scenarios. More advanced scenarios like handling exceptions during execution of method, handling large data, and more special cases need to be handled based on the actual requirement.
Note that the [PexMethod] attribute is used to specify the test method for Code Contracts. This attribute is used on the test method to indicate that the method is for testing the behavior of the code contracts. The test method will be run by the automatic testing tool when the code under test is rebuilt.

Note: This is a basic PEX usage and might not cover all scenarios. More advanced scenarios like handling exceptions during execution of method, handling large data, and more special cases need to be handled based on the actual requirement.

If you want to run the tests, you will have to add the TestClass attribute to the class declaration. That's how the test method will be detected by the testing tool.

Note: The actual testing tool would also need to be configured to use the test method. If you use ReSharper, you need to go to "ReSharper → Test → Run Settings → Edit..." and add the test method to the list of test methods to run.

If you want to run the tests automatically whenever the code under test is rebuilt, you can use the Code Contracts settings to update the testing tool when the code under test is built.

In conclusion: When testing with Code Contracts, make sure that the test method is properly annotated with [ContractAspect] attribute. The tooling will be able to automatically generate tests for public and private methods where the Code Contracts are defined. The tool can generate tests based on the pre-conditions and post-conditions defined in the contracts. The tool is smart enough to analyze the code, understand its semantics, and generate the tests that cover all the important behavior. If everything is set up right, the tooling can ensure that your code is correct and safe.

Note: For complex scenarios or particularly nuanced situations, you might need special handling to make PEX work, either with or without a custom test setup. The above guide is a general guideline and may need modifications based on the specific scenarios and requirements. So be sure to test the code thoroughly before--------------------            
