A: ArrayList's Insert method does not guarantee any particular order of elements after insertion if index points to a position that is not at the end of the list. It's merely a matter of adding the element at the end or at the desired position.
If you want to maintain a specific order, you'll need to use a different type of collection, possibly one of the sorted collections in .NET Framework or System.Collections.Generic namespace like SortedList.
Alternatively, if you simply want to know if order of elements matters to your specific problem, you can ask for a specification from your project manager. If it can be solved via a different design, it might not be necessary to use ArrayList.
Remember that ArrayList is just a dynamic array, it's not maintained in sorted way until you sort it.
ArrayList's Count will return the highest index of an item in the ArrayList plus one.
Please also note that generic List<T> in .NET Framework is recommended over ArrayList for its performance benefits.
Here is MSDN Link 
https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/4w8t8ehw.aspx
Hope this helps.

A: Given that the ArrayList.Insert method does not guarantee an order of elements after insertion, the conclusion is that "Inserting a new element into a particular index of an already sorted ArrayList will result in a reordering of the elements" is not a reliable statement about behavior. The provided code snippet demonstrates how the list may be changed after invoking the Insert method, but this cannot be reliably observed, due to the nature of the ArrayList class and its implementation.
ArrayList's Count property does not include the item being inserted but rather gives the next available index past the inserted item. This effectively means the item's index will be "index" when it gets inserted and it will be at index +1 when the insertion is finished. The ArrayList.Contains method, on the other hand, will immediately return true for an inserted item right after the insertion method call as it can directly access and check the inserted item’s index. The ArrayList.IndexOf and ArrayList.LastIndexOf can return the index of the recently inserted item as soon as it is available. However, if the ArrayList's behavior, or the ArrayList's items' order changes due to insertions other than being at the end, these methods might not reflect the recent changes accurately. Consequently, the observed result of these checks needs to be taken with more weight than usual when working with the Insert method's behavior in ArrayList.
In the provided code snippet, the order or arrangement of items in the ArrayList after an Insert operation depends on the other operations and method calls that were executed before or after the Insert operation. Additionally, the Insert operation could have the potential to change the items' order if another operation that impacts order was executed on the ArrayList before it. This makes it difficult to accurately assert how the ArrayList's state changed after an Insert operation. So, it's advisable to always test and verify the order of items in a situation like this one.
If the order of items after the Insert operation is important for the overall logic of your program, you may want to switch to a collection type that provides more control over order, like the SortedList or the List<T>. If the order of the items is not important, you can always rely on the Contains method for finding if the item was inserted successfully. Just as you have been doing already in your assertion.  
Overall, understanding and respecting the behavior of the method you are using, is crucial in creating reliable unit tests and overall functionality.

