        }
    }
}


A: The PexObserve.Invariant line is being triggered multiple times. When you're performing many test runs, you may find that every time there's an array list insert, this invariant condition fails because the old and new lists (arrList) are very close to each other in terms of content and structure, and therefore their Index of x, Last Index of x, and contains x comparison are always the same.
You could try to add another assumption that ensures there will be always an element in the array list at index that could be used. Alternatively, add a check before the insert action to see if x really will be included in arrList after insert operation (and ensure that the check is correct). In this way, you limit the possibilities of PexObserve.Invariant failing, but you still might have to manually check each inserted 'x'.
For example:
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Count > 0); // ensures there's at least one element in the list

    // other code...

    arrList.Insert(index, x);

    // other code...

    PexObserve.Invariant("{0} != {1} | {2} == {3} && {4} == {5}", Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);
}

This way you will most likely spot the incorrect element insertions due to the limitation in assumptions.
Remember that Pex testing is not a way to ensure correctness of the code but more like a form of automatic unit-testing based on all possible paths through the code. You still should manually test the program manually spotting any anomalies.
Good luck and let me know if I can also be of help ;)

A: Here is what you need to do:
In the current test, you use the PexObserve.Invariant construct with an expectation that the old and new lists are different, but in the real-world situation they might be identical. 
As a result, you need to use PexIgnoreOnReplay attribute. It instructs Pex to ignore the Invariant checks and stop generating tests until a fix is possible for them.
Apply this attribute to your PUT_InsertContract method to ignore the Invariant checks.

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexIgnoreOnReplay]
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    // your code...

    arrList.Insert(index, x);

    // your code...         

    PexObserve.Invariant("{0} != {1} | {2} == {3} && {4} == {5}", Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

}
```
In the Invariant string, you're trying to find that old and new lists are different. But in Pex, for every path that it explores, it checks the condition for every assertion it generates. Since all of the properties in your lists (Index of X, Last Index of X, and Contains) are always the same, Pex's Invariant always fails. PexIgnoreOnReplay instructs Pex to stop generating tests until you have a solution to this problem. Then you can remove it and re-run Pex again. 

Do remember that Pex is a tool to find all possible paths through the code but not a way to ensure that your tests are 100% correct. So it's always a good practice to use your own code as a second check.

Hope this helps and let me know if you have any other questions.



