            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("arrList after insertion: " + arrList);
        }

    }
}
```

This example demonstrates how to use PexObserve and PexAssert to analyze the state of your data, including the count and contents of a ArrayList before and after the Insert method is called. The test checks that the count increased by one upon insertion and that the new element is contained in the array list. It also uses PexObserve to output the values used in your testing to help you debug any issues.

The PexAssert framework has a great advantage when combined with PexObserve, and it becomes easier to understand exactly what is wrong in test failures. This way, you can visually see not only what the expected and actual values are, but also the sequence of values that caused the assertion to fail.

Remember that not every case that could cause a failure with this code may actually cause it. The code snippet given above tries to catch edge cases that can lead to incorrect insertions in the array list. So modify it according to the specific needs of your test case.


